docs/decisions.md:204:- **Q1.7** Where does release information live? — Stable release state and raw context; no safe references. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q6): release-context erasure is allowed only with a proof (release statically known, no per-instance information), including owning Text where applicable; no unconditional one-pointer claim.*
docs/decisions.md:212:- **Q2.2** Which containers implement `Destroy`? — Exactly sixteen named Destroy impls: Array (T: Destroy); Heap, HeapArray and Vec (two each: T: Copy; T: Destroy); String (unconditional); Option (T: Destroy); Result (three: T: Destroy/E: Copy, T: Destroy/E: Destroy, T: Copy/E: Destroy); OrderedMap (four: each of K and V Copy or Destroy). HashMap excluded. <a id="q2-2-revision"></a>Revised 2026-11-06 by [information flow](#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06): `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and their destruction wipes them.
docs/decisions.md:225:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the address is read from a read-only target profile; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile is unchanged, and the provenance rules and the zero-byte exception are preserved.*
docs/decisions.md:227:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): over the target profile's address spaces; the reconstruction policy is unchanged.*
docs/decisions.md:228:- **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the existing profile keeps the `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound; any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound.*
docs/decisions.md:236:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
docs/decisions.md:237:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
docs/decisions.md:242:- **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:243:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:249:- **Q7.1** How are target grant schemas and multiplicities specified? — One target, linux-x86_64-hosted (Linux ≥ 5.6), with a fixed grant table; main asks for grants as parameters. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): the grant table is a lawful extensible registry rather than a permanently closed one.*
docs/decisions.md:258:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): the grant table is a lawful extensible registry; the existing grant contracts are preserved, an unknown operation is refused, and the Entropy grant's shape waits for slice 6 ([question 14](#foundation-q14)).*
docs/decisions.md:273:- **Q9.7** How are primitive calls specified? — One exhaustive typed primitive-contract inventory. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; exhaustive coverage of the selected registry; an unknown operation is refused.*
docs/decisions.md:277:- **Q9.11** What do contracts mean and when are they checked? — Erased logical contracts; ensures sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup; no snapshot construct. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.*
docs/decisions.md:280:- **Q9.14** What must pass before Core freezes? — Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze. Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): the integration stage A2i (interaction proofs, semantic contract, modular layout, owner view, outside-reader pass) also passes before the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:285:- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:294:- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
docs/decisions.md:310:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
