docs/decisions.md:1286:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
docs/decisions.md:1402:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |

